Save states for Sega ST-V (Tecmo World Cup '98) and two MAME core gaps they showed: a machine
that loads a state goes on exactly as the one that saved it, whatever it ran before (another
player joining, a watcher, a resync). Found by diffing whole states item by item (straight run,
same machine after a load, a fresh machine after reset + load) and per-event logs.

- VDP1 (saturn_v.cpp): the two framebuffers (shown and drawn), the end-of-list registers (LOPR,
  COPR, EWDR) and the clip rectangles are saved; its post-load set the framebuffer
  configuration from the registers, which also made framebuffer 0 the drawn one and 1 the shown
  one: now it keeps the saved choice.
- VDP2: the dot clock and TV standard (dotsel, pal), the latched H/V counters and their flags,
  and the modes it last configured the screen and palette for (old_tvmd, old_crmd: a machine
  that doesn't know them configures the screen again on its next frame and its timing moves).
  After a load the SH-2s' clock follows the saved dot clock (dot_select_w sets it; device
  clocks aren't in save states: a machine loading one went on at 320 dots' clock).
- SMPC: INTBACK's status bits (the reset button's NMI enable, the dot clock) and the CD flag.
- ST-V (stv.cpp): the I/O chip's latches (port D's read-back: the game sets the coin counters
  and lockouts read-modify-write every frame; its mode, port G, the counters' bases, the serial
  byte), the A-bus protection's enable and key, and the backup RAM (zeros at power-on, not NVRAM
  on ST-V: the game keeps settings and play data there from the boot on).
- The A-bus (stv.cpp): stv_select_game copies the selected game's cartridge (48 MB) into the
  "abus" region the CPUs read it from, and memory regions aren't in states: a machine that loaded
  a state (its bank number already the saved one) never copied it and read zeros from the
  cartridge, which shows once the game streams data from it (through the 315-5881) a few seconds
  later. After a load the saved selection's cartridge is copied in.
- 315-5881 (315-5881_crypt.cpp): the rest of a stream's decryption and decompression
  (first_read, buffer_bit/bit2, buffer2/2a, block_size/pos/numlines, done_compression) is saved,
  and reset with the rest: a machine kept its own last stream's through a load and, most of
  it, a reset.
- SCSP: each slot's envelope phase (attack, decay 1/2, release; an int now, to be saveable) and
  its last sample (interpolation). Without them the sound CPU drifted even in the same machine.
- Scheduler (schedule.cpp): the active quanta (add_quantum's interleave boosts: twcup98 asks for
  5 us slices for 400 us on every MINIT/SINIT) are saved and made again on load.
- CPUs (devcpu.cpp, diexec.cpp): an access that a wait state deferred to the next timeslice
  (access_before_delay) remembers that its delay was counted by a tag, a handler's address in
  that machine, which no state carried: a machine loading a state taken with one pending counted
  the delay again, one 68000 cycle behind from there. The state saves whether there is one, and
  after a load the access being redone matches. Every CPU on the core gets these items, so every
  game's state layout changes (start-up states must be made again).
- libretro.cpp: RETRO_MEMORY_SYSTEM_RAM is the Saturn/ST-V high work RAM (workram_h), where the
  game runs: the largest-RAM rule took the low one, the same size, which barely changes in a game,
  so lockstep checkpoints couldn't see two machines drift apart.

Verified (mame/.cache/checks): states taken in the menus and in a match replay byte-identical
for 300 frames in the same machine, the same after a reset, and a fresh machine after reset +
load with any history before it; pictures identical from the second frame (the frontend draws a
fresh machine's first frame not at all, as always). emulator/netplay-check.mjs (real workers,
LOCKSTEP=1, two players joining, playing and leaving, a watcher) stays in step, also at 60 ms
one way with 25 ms jitter and 2% loss; before the A-bus and 315-5881 fixes the joiner drifted
from the host about 5 s after every handover (found by replaying the host's whole life offline
and bisecting it: the frame where the game selected its cartridge).

